<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kazembe</h1><p class="st-meta">1740 CE – 1899 CE (approx.) · capital Mwansabombwe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern Lunda kingdom of the Luapula valley, a wealthy trading state visited by Portuguese explorers and David Livingstone.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1740 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> general sent east by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwata-yamvo">Mwata Yamvo</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lunda-empire">Lunda Empire</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luapula-river">Luapula</a> valley south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mweru">Lake Mweru</a>. His successors, bearing the title Mwata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazembe">Kazembe</a>, built an independent kingdom that still paid tribute to the Lunda court.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazembe">Kazembe</a> ruled from a large capital through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> aristocrats who governed local chiefs. The kingdom traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves with Portuguese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tete">Tete</a> and Swahili-Arab merchants from the east coast. The Portuguese explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-lacerda-e-almeida">Francisco de Lacerda</a> died there in 1798, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">Livingstone</a> visited in 1867–68. The annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutomboko">Mutomboko ceremony</a> commemorates the conquest.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the late 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/msiri">Msiri's</a> Yeke state took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> and Swahili-Arab traders eroded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazembe">Kazembe's</a> power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> forces occupied the capital in 1899, and the kingdom was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>. The Mwata Kazembe remains a traditional ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1740 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.33%;width:3.16%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1740 CE – 1899 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mwansabombwe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kazembe III Lukwesa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Lunda religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lunda, Bemba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Zambia, Democratic Republic of the Congo</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lunda of Kazembe, Mwata Kazembe, Eastern Lunda</dd></dl></aside></div>

